From: "Pluet, LLC" <358065800+pluetllc@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sd_lavc: picture subtitles shown as text the app reads

Blu-ray (PGS) and DVD subtitles are pictures, so they ignored the subtitle
style (font, size, colours, position) that text tracks take. With
--sub-picture-text-callback (a function of the app's, passed as an integer
as --wid passes a window) and --sub-picture-text-context, sd_lavc hands each
picture it decodes to that function once: premultiplied BGRA (the union of
its parts), its start time, a hash of its pixels, the track's language and
the canvas size. The function copies what it needs and returns at once,
with the text when it knows it already; otherwise the text may come later
with the new sub-picture-text command (pts, hash, text). A picture with text
is drawn as that text by an sd_ass of its own (SubRip lines), so it takes
every option a text subtitle file takes; a picture in the canvas's top half
is drawn at the top ({\an8}). A picture without text is drawn as before, and
one drawn as a picture once stays a picture until it ends, so a line never
changes its look on screen.

Timing is the picture's: its start, and the end the next display set gives
(when that end arrives after the text went in, the text renderer's track is
rebuilt). A display set that repeats the one before up to its start (a
Blu-ray acquisition point) keeps its text, or its picture, without asking
again. While the app reads pictures, 16 decoded subtitles are kept instead
of 4, so lines are decoded, and read, well before they are due. Forced-only
playback (--sub-forced-events-only) keeps working: the picture holds only
its forced parts, and the text renderer does not drop them as it would for
a text track. DVD menus stay pictures. sub-text gives the read text.

Without the callback nothing changes (4 subtitles kept, pictures drawn).

---
